{"reference":"Jeremiah 9","book":"Jeremiah","chapter":9,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"In the wider book","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The opening cry for tears belongs to a wider pattern in Jeremiah, where images such as wounds, weeping, death without burial, displacement, and fractured memory portray catastrophe and survival. The received book also brings together poetry, prose sermons, narratives, letters, repeated accounts, restoration material, oracles concerning nations, and an appendix; its long compositional and textual history remains disputed. This wider setting helps place the chapter’s intense lament within a book that returns to devastation through several kinds of writing.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_009_4d764ec5ddc1efed4985e374"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}